      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the final price of the shirt after the successive discounts, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the sale price after the first discount:**\n   The original price of the shirt is $30. The first discount is 20%.\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Percentage discount} = 20\\%\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Sale price} = \\text{Original price} \\times (1 - \\text{Percentage discount})\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Sale price} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the final price after applying the second discount:**\n   Mary has a coupon that offers a 25% discount on the sale price.\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Percentage discount} = 25\\%\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final price} = \\text{Sale price} \\times (1 - \\text{Percentage discount})\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final price} = 24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n   \\]\n\n   So, the final price after both discounts is $18.\n\n3. **Calculate the overall percentage discount:**\n   The initial (regular) price of the shirt is $30, and the final price after both discounts is $18. The overall discount amount is:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = \\text{Initial price} - \\text{Final price}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 30 - 18 = 12\n   \\]\n\n   The overall percentage discount is the discount amount divided by the original price, scaled to 100%.\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Overall percentage discount} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{Discount amount}}{\\text{Original price}} \\right) \\times 100\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Overall percentage discount} = \\left( \\frac{12}{30} \\right) \\times 100 = 0.4 \\times 100 = 40\\%\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final single percent discount that gives the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
